        <referenceFunction amount="1.0" category="construction" datasetRelatesToProduct="true" generalComment="Refers to foam glass of a density of 110 kg/m3 and a thermal conductivity of 0.040 W/mK. Foam glass products sold to Switzerland and Lichtenstein are produced with renewable energy certificates from RECS (www.recs.org). ;&#10;Synonyms: T4 WDS; &#10;UUID: de6ba86b-50ab-3ab2-8208-6c112921a8f8" includedProcesses="melting, forming to a pipe, cooling down, smashing, crushing, mixing with carbon powder. Foaming by heating in a mould, cooling, cutting to slabs and packing. Internal processes (workshop, administration etc.), transport of raw materials and energy carrier for furnace, packing and infrastructure are also included." infrastructureProcess="false" localCategory="construction" localName="Foam glass, at plant" localSubCategory="insulation" name="Foam glass, at plant" subCategory="insulation" unit="kg"/>
        <geography location="RER" text="Data are from one company in Belgium which covers nearly 100% of the European foam glass market. For some exchanges RER-, CH- and GLO-modules are used as proxy. No energy certificates are accounted"/>
        <technology text="All data refer to the year 2005. Today foam glass is made of 68% recycled glass and only recycled glass is used instead of silica sand. Recycled glass is to some extent directly injected to the cellulating furnace without melting, resulting in less energy use. The energy for the melting process is electricity. Natural gas is used for the cellulating process. All emissions from processing raw materials and heating are taken into account."/>
